============================================================
MEMBER 1 — MANUAL
NEW CONCEPT: SHARED VALIDATION
============================================================

Files involved:

hospital/js/validation.js
hospital/js/auth.js
hospital/js/signup.js


1. RESPONSIBILITY OF validation.js

validation.js contains reusable validation rules.

It does NOT:
- handle login
- create accounts
- manage sessions
- access patient data
- access billing data

Its responsibility is only to answer whether
input satisfies our application's validation rules.


2. WHY SHARED VALIDATION?

Both Login and Sign Up need to validate:

- User ID
- Password

Instead of writing the same validation rules in both
auth.js and signup.js, we put the rules in validation.js.

The relationship is:

                    validation.js
                    /          \
                   /            \
              auth.js        signup.js

This means that if a validation rule changes,
we only need to change it in one place.


3. FUNCTIONS

isValidUserId(userId)

Checks:
- minimum 8 characters
- letters and numbers only

Returns:
true  -> valid
false -> invalid


isValidPassword(password)

Checks:
- minimum 10 characters
- uppercase letter
- lowercase letter
- number
- special character

Returns:
true  -> valid
false -> invalid


4. REGULAR EXPRESSIONS

We use regular expressions for simple pattern checks.

Example:

/^[A-Za-z0-9]+$/

This means:
- A-Z -> uppercase letters
- a-z -> lowercase letters
- 0-9 -> numbers
- + -> one or more characters

Therefore it allows:
admission01
admin1234

but rejects:
admin_123
admin@123


5. SCRIPT LOADING ORDER

index.html loads:

<script src="js/session.js"></script>
<script src="js/user-data.js"></script>
<script src="js/validation.js"></script>
<script src="js/auth.js"></script>

The order matters.

JavaScript files loaded with normal <script> tags
execute in the order they appear.

auth.js uses:
- createSession() from session.js
- findUserById() from user-data.js
- isValidUserId() from validation.js
- isValidPassword() from validation.js

Therefore these dependencies must be loaded before auth.js.

The same idea applies to signup.html:

<script src="js/user-data.js"></script>
<script src="js/validation.js"></script>
<script src="js/signup.js"></script>

signup.js uses functions from user-data.js
and validation.js, so they are loaded first.


6. SRP INSIGHT

A common beginner mistake is putting all logic into one file.

For example:

auth.js could technically contain:
- validation
- user lookup
- password checking
- session creation
- localStorage handling

But that would give auth.js too many responsibilities.

Instead we currently have:

auth.js
    -> authentication

user-data.js
    -> user data

validation.js
    -> validation rules

session.js
    -> session management

signup.js
    -> account creation

Each component has a clearer responsibility.


7. IMPORTANT SECURITY INSIGHT

Our prototype currently stores passwords directly
in localStorage.

Example:

{
    userId: "admission01",
    password: "Hospital@1"
}

This is NOT secure.

Anyone with browser developer tools can inspect
localStorage.

We are doing this only because this is a
browser-only learning prototype with no backend.

A real hospital application should not store passwords
this way. Real applications use a backend and secure
password hashing.

Therefore:

WORKING PROTOTYPE != PRODUCTION-READY SECURITY


8. SIGN UP VS SESSION CREATION

Our current flow is intentionally:

Sign Up
    ->
Create account
    ->
Store user
    ->
Ask user to Login
    ->
Authenticate
    ->
Create session

We could technically create the session immediately
after Sign Up.

We are not doing that currently because:

signup.js
    -> account creation

auth.js
    -> authentication

session.js
    -> session management

are separate responsibilities.

This gives us a practical example of SRP.


