10. IMPORTANT RULE FOR THE TEAM

Do not duplicate validation rules in individual files
when a shared validation function already exists.

Use:

isValidUserId()
isValidPassword()

instead of creating another version of the same rules.

============================================================
NEW CONCEPT: LOGIN ATTEMPTS AND TEMPORARY LOCKOUT
============================================================

New file:

hospital/js/login-attempts.js


1. RESPONSIBILITY

login-attempts.js is responsible for:

- counting failed login attempts
- determining whether login is locked
- determining remaining attempts
- determining lockout expiry
- resetting failed attempts


It does NOT:

- authenticate passwords
- create users
- create sessions
- display the login form


2. CONSTANTS

MAX_LOGIN_ATTEMPTS = 3

The maximum number of failed attempts allowed.

LOCKOUT_DURATION_MINUTES = 1

How long the temporary lockout lasts.

We use 1 minute during development because it
makes testing easier.


3. WHERE LOCKOUT DATA IS STORED

The prototype stores login attempt information
in localStorage.

Key:

loginAttempts


Example:

{
    "failedAttempts": 2,
    "lockedUntil": null
}


localStorage is used because normal JavaScript
variables disappear when the page is refreshed.


4. IMPORTANT SECURITY / DESIGN NOTE

This is a simplified browser-only prototype.

The lockout implementation is NOT a production-grade
authentication/security system.

A real authentication system would normally have
a backend and server-side account protection.

We are using this implementation to learn:

- state
- localStorage
- JSON
- functions
- time calculations
- separation of responsibilities


5. AUTHENTICATION FLOW

Login flow is now:

User submits login
        ↓
Check lockout
        ↓
Validate User ID
        ↓
Validate Password
        ↓
Find user
        ↓
Check password
        ↓
     ┌─────────────┐
     │             │
  Success        Failure
     │             │
     ↓             ↓
Reset attempts  Record attempt
     │             │
     ↓             ↓
Create session  Check lockout
                   │
                   ↓
              Show remaining
              attempts / lockout


6. SRP INSIGHT

auth.js does not contain the lockout algorithm.

Instead it calls:

recordFailedLogin()
isLoginLocked()
getRemainingAttempts()
resetLoginAttempts()

These functions belong to login-attempts.js.

This means auth.js coordinates the login process
while login-attempts.js manages lockout state.

This is a practical example of SRP.


7. SCRIPT ORDER

index.html currently loads:

<script src="js/session.js"></script>
<script src="js/user-data.js"></script>
<script src="js/validation.js"></script>
<script src="js/login-attempts.js"></script>
<script src="js/auth.js"></script>

The order matters because auth.js uses functions
defined in the earlier files.

Dependency order:

session.js
    ↓
user-data.js
    ↓
validation.js
    ↓
login-attempts.js
    ↓
auth.js


8. TIME HANDLING

JavaScript Date objects are used to calculate
the lockout expiry time.

Conceptually:

current time
    +
lockout duration
    =
lockedUntil


Example:

Current time: 10:00
Lockout duration: 1 minute
lockedUntil: 10:01


The application then compares:

current time < lockedUntil

If true:
login remains locked.

If false:
lockout has expired.


9. JSON INSIGHT

localStorage stores strings.

Therefore we use:

JSON.stringify()

when saving an object.

Example:

localStorage.setItem(
    "loginAttempts",
    JSON.stringify(loginAttempts)
);


When reading it back:

JSON.parse()

converts the stored string back into
a JavaScript object.

Example:

const loginAttempts =
    JSON.parse(data);


