Observe when the lockout occurs.


Experiment 2:
Change LOCKOUT_DURATION_MINUTES from 1 to 2.

Observe how long the lockout lasts.


Experiment 3:
Open Developer Tools -> Application ->
Local Storage.

Inspect loginAttempts after failed logins.


Experiment 4:
Refresh the page while locked.

Observe whether the lockout remains.

This demonstrates why localStorage was used instead
of a normal JavaScript variable.


Experiment 5:
Successfully login after failed attempts.

Inspect localStorage and observe that loginAttempts
has been reset.


Experiment 6:
Temporarily remove the call to resetLoginAttempts()
after successful login.

Observe what happens to the failed attempt state.


11. CURRENT LIMITATION

The current prototype stores one shared loginAttempts
object.

Therefore failed attempts are not yet tracked
separately for every user.

A more advanced version could store:

{
    "admission01": {
        failedAttempts: 2,
        lockedUntil: null
    },
    "admission02": {
        failedAttempts: 1,
        lockedUntil: null
    }
}

We are intentionally NOT implementing this yet
because it is not necessary for the current
learning step.


12. IMPORTANT PRINCIPLE

Do not add complexity simply because a production
system might eventually need it.

First make the simple requirement work.

Then improve the design when the requirement
actually requires it.


STEP — Login Lockout Countdown

File:
hospital/js/auth.js

New responsibility:
Display the remaining lockout time to the user.

Function:
startLockoutCountdown()

How it works:

1. login-attempts.js stores the lockout end time.
2. auth.js calls getLockoutEndTime().
3. Date.now() gives the current time in milliseconds.
4. The difference between the two gives remaining time.
5. Math.ceil() converts the remaining milliseconds into seconds.
6. setInterval() runs the countdown every 1 second.
7. clearInterval() stops the countdown when the lockout ends.

Important SRP rule:

login-attempts.js owns lockout DATA and lockout RULES.

auth.js owns the LOGIN PAGE BEHAVIOR and DISPLAY.

Do not move all lockout logic into auth.js just because the countdown is displayed there.

Important JavaScript concept:

setInterval(function, 1000)

means:
"Run this function approximately every 1000 milliseconds."

clearInterval(timer)

stops that repeated execution.

window.lockoutTimer is used so that we can keep track of the
currently running countdown and prevent multiple countdowns
from running at the same time.

Experiment:

Change:

setInterval(updateCountdown, 1000)

to:

setInterval(updateCountdown, 500)

The countdown will update twice as frequently.

The displayed seconds will still decrease approximately once
per second because the actual calculation is based on Date.now(),
not on the number of interval executions.

Why Date.now() is better than simply doing:

remainingSeconds--;

Because the browser can delay JavaScript execution.
Calculating the difference between actual times is more reliable.

Current limitation:

The lockout is currently global in this prototype.

That means the same loginAttempts object is used for all users.

A production system would normally track attempts per account,
and the backend would enforce the lockout.

IMPORTANT JAVASCRIPT CONCEPT — Date vs Timestamp

The application stores lockedUntil as an ISO date string:

"2026-09-13T12:15:30.000Z"

This is useful because it is human-readable and easy to
store as JSON.

Before performing time calculations, convert it to a
timestamp number:

new Date(dateString).getTime()

A timestamp is simply a number representing a point in time.

This allows calculations such as:

lockoutEndTime - Date.now()

The NaN problem happened because the countdown expected a
numeric timestamp but getLockoutEndTime() was returning the
ISO string directly.

SRP remains unchanged:

recordFailedLogin()
    Creates and stores the lockout time.

getLockoutEndTime()
    Reads and converts the lockout time.

auth.js
    Uses the converted value to display the countdown.

STEP — Protected Dashboard and Logout

Files introduced:

hospital/dashboard.html
hospital/js/dashboard.js

dashboard.html:
Provides the first protected page after login.

dashboard.js:
Responsible for dashboard-specific behavior.

It performs two main tasks:

1. Check whether a valid session exists.
2. Handle logout.

Session protection:

if (!isSessionValid()) {
    window.location.href = "index.html";
}

This means:
"If the current session is not valid, return to login."

If the session is valid:

getCurrentUser()

retrieves the logged-in user's ID.

The ID is then displayed on the dashboard.

Logout:

logout()

removes the session information.

After logout:

window.location.href = "index.html";

returns the user to the login page.

Important concept:

A protected page should not assume that the user arrived
there through the login page.

A user can directly type:

dashboard.html

into the browser.

Therefore dashboard.js checks the session when the page loads.

Script loading order:

dashboard.html loads:

session.js
        ↓
dashboard.js

dashboard.js depends on functions from session.js,
such as:

isSessionValid()
getCurrentUser()
logout()

Therefore session.js must be loaded first.

Login flow:

auth.js
    ↓
createSession()
    ↓
dashboard.html
    ↓
dashboard.js
    ↓
isSessionValid()
    ↓
show dashboard OR redirect to login

Logout flow:

dashboard.js
    ↓
logout()
    ↓
session destroyed
    ↓
index.html

Current limitation:

This is frontend-only session protection.

sessionStorage can be manipulated by the user.
It is suitable for this learning prototype, but not
for real hospital security.

Experiment:

After logging in, open Developer Tools and inspect
sessionStorage.

You should see the session information created by
session.js.

Then click Logout and inspect sessionStorage again.

The session should be gone.

Another experiment:

Open dashboard.html directly after logging out.

The page should redirect to index.html.


