STEP — Reusable Application Shell

File:

hospital/js/app-shell.js

Purpose:

Provide the common application UI used by all protected pages.

Responsibilities:

createHeader()
createSidebar()
createFooter()
setupSidebarToggle()
setupLogout()

The HTML pages no longer contain the actual header/sidebar/footer
content.

Instead they contain placeholders:

<header id="appHeader"></header>

<aside id="sidebar"></aside>

<footer id="appFooter"></footer>

app-shell.js fills those elements using innerHTML.

Important concept:

innerHTML allows JavaScript to insert HTML content into
an existing HTML element.

Architecture:

Page HTML
    ↓
Provides placeholders + feature content

app-shell.js
    ↓
Provides common UI

style.css
    ↓
Provides common appearance


SRP:

dashboard.js:
    Protects the dashboard.

app-shell.js:
    Creates and controls the common shell.

session.js:
    Manages sessions.

These are separate responsibilities.

Dependency order:

session.js
    ↓
dashboard.js
    ↓
app-shell.js

session.js must load first because both other files use
functions defined there.


Important team rule:

Feature owners should primarily work inside:

<main class="main-content">

They should not duplicate or redesign the common header,
sidebar, or footer unless a common-shell requirement changes.

This allows Member 1 to maintain the common UI in one place.

COMMON SHELL CONTRACT

Every protected application page should contain:

<header id="appHeader"></header>

<aside id="sidebar"></aside>

<footer id="appFooter"></footer>

and load:

session.js
dashboard.js
app-shell.js

The page-specific functionality belongs inside:

<main class="main-content">

app-shell.js creates the common UI.

Feature pages should not duplicate the header,
sidebar, or footer.

Sidebar navigation is maintained centrally by Member 1.

Changes to the common sidebar should normally be made
in app-shell.js rather than separately in every page.



